Only way to do this without triggering CDP is by fooling chrome into thinking it's a mobile device with width: 1920 and height: 1080.

background.js (build a chrome extension or inject with a automated browser framework)

```

const config = {
    width: 1920,
    height: 1080,
    mobile: true,
    deviceScaleFactor: 1
};

chrome.tabs.onUpdated.addListener(async (tabId, info, tab) => {

  if (!tab?.url) return;

  if (
    tab.url.startsWith("chrome://") ||
    tab.url.startsWith("devtools://") ||
    tab.url.startsWith("chrome-extension://")
  ) return;

  try {
    await chrome.debugger.attach({ tabId }, "1.3");

    await chrome.debugger.sendCommand(
      { tabId },
      "Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride",
      {
        width: config.width,
        height: config.height,
        deviceScaleFactor: config.deviceScaleFactor,
        mobile: config.mobile,
        fitWindow: true
      }
    );

  } catch (e) {
  }
});
```

If you change mobile: false and set a custom width or height some websites might detect that the chrome dev tools are open. Other than this I dont know if it's possible to do in chrome since the screen dimensions is something that runs outside of chrome. Maybe start a virtual monitor for each profile but then we not any longer talking about it being a chrome only project  but user also need to have virtual monitor drivers installed on his computer as well.........

